WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.200
Mitigation should be risk-based, tested, and operationally realistic.

00:00:04.420 --> 00:00:10.900
Post-quantum cryptography is one part of the response, especially for public-key cryptographic roles that may be vulnerable in the future.

00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:17.200
But PQC adoption should be standards-based and implemented through mature libraries and supported protocols.

00:00:17.420 --> 00:00:28.140
Teams should avoid custom cryptography, unsupported algorithm choices, and private protocol designs, because unreviewed mechanisms can create new risk while trying to solve future risk.

00:00:28.360 --> 00:00:30.760
Crypto agility is another key mitigation.

00:00:30.980 --> 00:00:37.700
A crypto-agile system can change algorithms, keys, libraries, or protocols without redesigning the whole architecture.

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:44.880
That requires configuration discipline, versioned dependencies, clear ownership, test environments, rollback plans, and monitoring.

00:00:45.100 --> 00:00:51.820
Hybrid cryptography may also appear during transition periods, conceptually combining classical and post-quantum mechanisms.

00:00:52.040 --> 00:00:57.560
Hybrid approaches still require standards-aware implementation, compatibility testing, and governance.

00:00:57.780 --> 00:01:00.580
Data lifecycle management can reduce exposure.

00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:07.520
If sensitive data is not retained unnecessarily, it cannot be harvested from that location for long-term future exposure.

00:01:07.740 --> 00:01:15.740
Retention review, minimization, segmentation, access control, secure deletion processes, and archive governance all contribute.

00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:22.120
Key rotation may help in some contexts, but teams should understand what rotation does and does not protect.

00:01:22.340 --> 00:01:28.660
Changing keys does not automatically erase risk from traffic or data already captured under older assumptions.

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:31.920
Practical migration uses pilots and staged deployment.

00:01:32.140 --> 00:01:40.940
Teams can start with lower-risk systems, test interoperability, measure performance, review logging, coordinate with vendors, and document results.

00:01:41.160 --> 00:01:51.080
Vendor readiness tracking is important because many cryptographic dependencies live in cloud services, products, libraries, appliances, and platforms outside direct control.

00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:57.940
The best mitigation program combines cryptographic migration with data minimization, operational testing, and clear decision records.
